ield.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f729bcf8-f875-5e33-9fc0-12814c32c7be" :question/math-academy-id "q-104981" :question/problem "![](images/91/916c7fa803c4fb2949cfc9a7f1c8fd05dde8cfe004673eb22f313daeb89083be.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-1,4]\\to [-1,4]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[0,4]$\n- $∀\\,y\\in [-1,4],$ $∃\\,x\\in [-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the highest $y$-value is $4$ and the lowest $y$-value is $0.$ The graph covers all $y$-values between $0$ and $4$, including these numbers, so the range of the function is $[0,4].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider, for example,  $y=-1.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x\\in [-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $[-1,4],$ there exists some $x$ contained within $[-1,4]$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $[-1,4]$ onto $[-1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152135 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6fa67074-d3c8-5000-95a3-7f935936ab7d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surjections For Functions Defined Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d092e443-3e79-5312-a656-63c615a63f0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ide