wer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8b3a8c0-a6c1-578d-9257-3e5ed98ce4ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471255} :answer-field/id #uuid "78d00a60-493e-5993-9de4-453b1082e9fc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9947189b-8991-5f0c-ad01-e90d4c094369" :question/math-academy-id "q-183507" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P(z):$ ${e}^{z}<0$ or ${z}^{4}\\le -1.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $R,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The truth set of $P(z)$ is $∅.$\n- **II.** The truth set of $P(z)$ is $R.$\n- **III.** $∀z\\in R,\\,P(z).$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${e}^{z}<0$\" is $∅,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${z}^{4}\\le -1$\" is $∅.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=∅\\cup ∅=∅.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The truth set is empty.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, ${T}_{P}\\ne R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ does *not* coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀z\\in R,\\,P(z)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/r