ages/86/86568c2024cfeb7f6605cd43a92f29492e24a1366216f51aa238c8c339bcff59.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- The range of $f$ is $[2,\\infty )$\n- $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n- $f$ is a surjection\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection (also known as *onto*) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is *at least one* element in $X$ that maps to $y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that the lowest $y$-value is $y=2,$ and it occurs at the point $(-2,2).$ The graph covers all $y$-values greater than $y=2,$ including $2$ itself.  So, the range of the function is $[2,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Consider $y=0.$ As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $y$ contained within $R,$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a surjection of $R$ onto $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152133 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63a2b195-d0b3-580c-9230-58533d86afd5" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Domain and Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f01503f6-fc6a-50e4-a24c-82f19ac86d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471585} {:answer/id #uuid "78710886-40f8-5c1b-bb4c-05340d1f4065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uu