b/id #edb/ref 17592186471334} :answer-field/id #uuid "9104dafe-72f4-5668-810d-9347cbc93cc9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "576618c9-5633-5684-823b-e6a81214839e" :question/math-academy-id "q-216041" :question/problem "Let $x\\in Q.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $______,$ then $\\sqrt{x}\\in C.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n- **I.** $\\frac{x}{5}\\in Q$\n- **II.** $x>0$\n- **III.** $|x|<-1$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $x\\in Q,$ is always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $x\\in Q,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $x\\in Q,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157233 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f828cb-c8b1-5da4-9711-15ace712dffa" :knowledge-point/title "Completing Vacuous Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30b39e76-bc8c-52a1-9ab4-8b53fc905cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471335} {:answer/id #uuid "cff96380-a1c0-587a-987e-3f2acfcaf2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b3